## Branch rules
<!-- Sigma's own standing rule, not a project choice - see docs/branching-model.md in the plugin -->
- **Nobody commits directly to a feature branch. All work reaches it through `sdlc/*` goal
  branches.** For a unit: the `sdlc/<goal-id>` branch cut for a goal, then a pull request into
  `feature/<name>`. Rebasing a shared branch rewrites published history; that is safe only while
  nobody is holding commits on it.
- Sigma **detects** a violation and stops bringing the branch forward — it cannot prevent one.
  It checks only on a pick, and only when the feature branch is behind its base; when it skips it
  reports nothing, so silence is not evidence the rule held.
- Only host branch protection prevents a direct commit — but protecting `feature/*` in the ordinary
  way also blocks the force-push upkeep needs, stopping upkeep entirely. Protect your integration
  branch; on `feature/*`, grant upkeep's actor a bypass.
- To rebase, catch up or land a unit (a `feature/<name>` branch), use the `sigma-rebase` skill; do not run a raw rebase, force-push or merge by hand.

